<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Zollfeld</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Zollfeld is a plain in the valley of the Glan, north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/klagenfurt">Klagenfurt</a> in the Austrian state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carinthia">Carinthia</a>. It was the site of the Roman city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virunum">Virunum</a>, capital of the province of Noricum, and later the centre of Slavic Carantania. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dukes-chair">Duke's Chair</a>, used for the installation of the dukes of Carinthia, still stands there.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zollfeld.jpg" alt="Zollfeld" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Griensteidl · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zollfeld_Blick_von_Karnburg_nach_Maria_Saal.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Zollfeld, in Slovene Gosposvetsko polje, is a slightly ascending plain with small woods, hills and swamps, and one of the oldest cultural landscapes in the East Alpine region. It lies in the Klagenfurt Basin of the Central Eastern Alps and runs along the Glan River from north of Klagenfurt to Sankt Veit an der Glan. It is about 18 km long and between 400 m and 2 km wide, at an elevation of 450 to 455 m above sea level.</p><p>Four peaks of the basin surround the plain: the Ulrichsberg (1,022 m) to the south, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magdalensberg">Magdalensberg</a> (1,059 m) to the east, and the Gößeberg (1,171 m) and the Lorenziberg (971 m) to the north. For about 500 years the four mountains have been stops on the Vierbergelauf, a procession held each year on the second Friday after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/easter">Easter</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The oldest archaeological finds on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magdalensberg">Magdalensberg</a> date from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hallstatt-culture">Hallstatt culture</a> of the 8th to 6th centuries BCE. The area was the cultural and political centre of the Celtic kingdom of Noricum and of the later Roman province. Under Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claudius">Claudius</a> (41–54) the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virunum">Virunum</a> was founded as the provincial capital, replacing ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/noreia">Noreia</a> or perhaps identical with it. Excavations in the Roman cemetery of Virunum began in 2015.</p><p>After the Slavic settlement of the Eastern Alps around 600, the fortress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnburg">Karnburg</a> (Krnski grad) became the centre of the Principality of Carantania, which Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odilo-of-bavaria">Odilo of Bavaria</a> made a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> around 740. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> was part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carolingian-empire">Carolingian Empire</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a> from 788, and an imperial palace, a Kaiserpfalz, was built at Karnburg around 830. It remained the administrative centre after the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carinthia">Carinthia</a> was split off from Bavaria in 976.</p><p>Inside the castle was a meeting place where the dukes of Carantania were installed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/princes-stone">Prince's Stone</a>, the base of an ancient Roman Ionic column. Near Maria Saal stands the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dukes-chair">Duke's Chair</a>, at which the newly installed dukes distributed their land among their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Zollfeld” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After Samo's death around 658 they formed an independent principality centred on the <b>Zollfeld</b> plain near Karnburg in modern Carinthia.</blockquote><a href="/state/carantania">Carantania</a></li><li><blockquote>Dukes were installed at the Prince's Stone near Karnburg and the Duke's Chair on the <b>Zollfeld</b>, in a ceremony partly in Slovene.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-carinthia">Duchy of Carinthia</a></li></ul></section>
